      "rationale": "Each of the four tables is actually exercised rather than assumed: sixes through 6 × 6 and 6 × 7, sevens through 4 × 7 and 5 × 7, eights through 8 × 8 and 3 × 8, and nines through 4 × 9 and 8 × 9. The array tasks reach the larger entries such as the 9×8 rectangle."
    },
    "cognitive_capacity": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "Single-fact recall with one-line prompts is appropriate for Grade 3 Quarter 3, and the shading task 'Shade all the squares inside the 4×6 rectangle' supplies a concrete route for pupils who do not yet have the fact memorised."
    },
    "variant_comprehensiveness": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "The node spans question form, equation form, truth checking, a marked worked example, an array to read, a rectangle to shade, and one grouping story about water bowls in bags. Presentation is not confined to a single template."
    },
    "competency_alignment": {
      "verdict": "PASS",
      "rationale": "The sequence climbs from an identity-like 1 × 6 through mid-table facts to 8 × 9 and the 9×8 shading, so what gets harder is the difficulty of the fact itself rather than the surrounding language. Two seeds do land on the same 4 × 9 item with only the option order changed, which is a variety blemish rather than a misdirected axis."
    },
